Reading list: small base programs and scripts worth reading on this machine

For each: its manual page first, then the source (less or vi), then a
copy under ~/src/learn to change and break. Shell first, then C.

Shell (ksh), installed with the system: nothing to fetch.

  /etc/netstart           Brings network interfaces up from
                          /etc/hostname.IF files: small functions, case,
                          `while read` over a file.
  /etc/rc                 The boot script, run by init(8). Long, but a
                          list of steps: read the functions at the top,
                          then follow the order at the bottom.
  /etc/rc.d/rc.subr       What every daemon script in /etc/rc.d shares.
                          Read one of them (/etc/rc.d/sshd) beside it.
  /usr/sbin/sysupgrade    sysupgrade(8): getopts, an err() function, and
                          checking the downloaded sets with signify(1)
                          before trusting them.
  /usr/sbin/rcctl         rcctl(8): getopts, and editing
                          /etc/rc.conf.local through a mktemp file.
  /usr/sbin/sysmerge      sysmerge(8): merges configuration files after
                          an upgrade. The longest; read it last.

C, in /usr/src (fetch it first: see below). All follow style(9), and
each calls pledge(2) before it does any work.

  /usr/src/usr.bin/yes/yes.c      The smallest: a pledge and a loop.
  /usr/src/bin/echo/echo.c        Options parsed by hand: a comment
                                  forbids getopt(3). Work out why.
  /usr/src/bin/cat/cat.c          getopt(3), files or standard input,
                                  err(3), a second, narrower pledge.
  /usr/src/usr.bin/head/head.c    Numbers from options with strtonum(3).
  /usr/src/usr.bin/wc/wc.c        Counting over several files, a total.

Each directory also holds a Makefile for bsd.prog.mk (as
`learn new NAME c` makes) and the manual page.

Getting /usr/src

/usr/src is empty until the source is fetched. cvs(1) is in base, and it
is the way for both -current and a release. Commands from memory of the
OpenBSD FAQ (www.openbsd.org/faq/faq5.html is the authority; mirrors and
their host key fingerprints are on www.openbsd.org/anoncvs.html).

  Once, as root: /usr/src belongs to group wsrc, so let your user write
  to it with `usermod -G wsrc YOU`, then log in again.

  -current, what this machine runs on snapshots until 8.0 ships: the
  main branch (no -r).

      cd /usr
      cvs -qd anoncvs@anoncvs.ca.openbsd.org:/cvs checkout -P src

  8.0-stable, once the machine runs the 8.0 release: the release's
  branch, so the source matches the system plus its errata fixes.

      cd /usr
      cvs -qd anoncvs@anoncvs.ca.openbsd.org:/cvs checkout -rOPENBSD_8_0 -P src

  Either way, `cd /usr/src && cvs -q up -Pd` updates it later; after
  moving the machine from snapshots to 8.0, `cvs -q up -Pd -rOPENBSD_8_0`
  switches the same checkout to the branch.
